========= Per Thread Page Tables (ARM) ===================
The goal is to convert critical sections from 
  lock(var) 
      critical_section
  unlock(var)

to 
   store(var)             // No CAS no fence just a plain store
      critical_section
   store(var)             // no #storeload / mfence or dmb style fence
                          // just a plain store

Why? 
Some CPUs on ARM with write-through/read allocate dcaches do very poorly on back to back atomics and cause pipeline latencies. This is 
true even when that line is held by that core in E state. And depending on memory model some of them require additional line invalidates/
fences in lock(var). 

============
Goal
============
-> improve uncontested lock performance 
   <avoid CAS/fence/stalls for back to back locks by the same thread>
-> make __no worse__  the "Read Often" case 
   <aka cases where data is read all the time but written to rarely>

========================
Implementation:
========================
On Linux task_struct -> mm_struct -> pgd_t* points to first level page directory. We want to maintain several pgd_ts
all of which are mirrors of each other except they differ in a single last level page table or a few ptes.

<a> Initially the page starts up read only.  

<b> On first write access thread faults in fault handler tries to
acquire the real lock that is hashed on faulting page address. If
successful, it modifies a copy of the page table and gives itself
write permission for that page and henceforth uses this pgd.  

<c> It is possible it cannot immediately acquire the lock and in that case it can sleep
or spin on the real lock. Once acquired subsequent lock acquires are free on that same thread.

<d> any other thread entering its critical section will fault and try
to acquire the real lock. 

=================
TLB Invalidates
=================
As you can imagine there is an explicit lock handover taking place here so only 1 IPI to a single core is required in addition to 
invalidating local TLB. Avoids global invalidate broadcasts. During handover we might also need to stick a #storeload or DMB style
fence on the old cpu.

=================
Thread Migration
=================
A thread that holds this lock is biased to that core. While migrating CPU scheduler needs to be modified to use this as a deterrent.
If it does migrate that thread then pre migration we ll need a local TLB invalidate and post migration another invalidate will be required
but in both cases they would be on local cores so hopefully it is cheap.

=================
Memory wastage
=================
This is true! We re asking for a page per lock variable. Need to see if we can improve this by pushing similar locks to a page.

